async function triggerDR() {
  await promoteGlobalWriter('us-west-2');
  await updateRoute53Records('us-west-2');
  await validateFailover();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account data governance at scale beyond single-service configs.

## Key Concepts

- Lake Formation cross-account data sharing
- IAM roles with trusted principals and external IDs
- RAM for resource sharing
- KMS cross-account encryption
- EventBridge/Glue orchestration
- Auditing and revocation testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: assume cross-account role and write a marker to shared S3
const { STSClient, AssumeRoleCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-sts");
const { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

async function testAccess(roleArn, bucket, key, body) {
  const sts = new STSClient({});
  const r = await sts.send(new AssumeRoleCommand({ RoleArn: roleArn, RoleSessionName: "AuditTest" }));
  const s3 = new S3Client({ credentials: {
    accessKeyId: r.Credentials.AccessKeyId,
    secretAccessKey: r.Credentials.SecretAccessKey,
    sessionToken: r.Credentials.SessionToken
  }});
  await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key, Body: body }));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test and enforce revocation across all accounts?
- How would you verify no data exposure in an incident scenario across accounts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end AWS streaming design, data locality, replay semantics, and observability across regions.

## Key Concepts
- Data locality, replay, idempotency, cross-region replication, shard throttling, and cost trade-offs.
- Durability vs latency in KDS/Kafka vs Firehose patterns.
- Observability via CloudWatch, X-Ray, and structured metrics.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent sink to DynamoDB
async function sinkEvent(event){
  const key = event.tenantId + '#' + event.id;
  await dynamo.put({
    TableName: 'TenantEvents',
    Item: { PK: key, ...event },
    ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(PK)'
  }).catch(err => {
    if (err.code === 'ConditionalCheckFailedException') return; // idempotent retry
    throw err
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle shard throttling and bursty traffic?
- How would you onboard new tenants without impacting latency or isolation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical AWS multi-tenant isolation at scale, combining IAM session tags, Lake Formation, data catalog usage, and automated revocation; probes policy design, GitOps deployment, and testing.

## Key Concepts
- IAM role session tags
- Lake Formation permissions
- Data catalog integration
- Event-driven revocation with DynamoDB, EventBridge, Step Functions
- GitOps deployment of IAM policies
- Auditability: CloudTrail, Config, Access Analyzer

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy snippet (AssumeRole requires matching tenant-id tag)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"
        }
      },
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/PrincipalRoleForTenant"}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy snippet (tenant-scoped S3 and Lake Formation actions)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["lakeformation:GetDataAccess"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"lakeformation:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-tenant leakage with automated CI checks?
- What monitoring would you add to detect revocation failures and alert on latency breaches?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of end-to-end data ingestion, least-privilege IAM, event-driven design, and basic data validation in Lambda.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event triggers and signed URLs
- IAM least privilege for Lambda roles
- SQS integration with Lambda
- DynamoDB PutItem and idempotent patterns
- Simple data validation and error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Ingest Lambda header validation (pseudo)
function validHeader(csv){ 
  const exp = ['id','name','amount','date'];
  const head = csv.split('\n')[0].split(',').map(x=>x.trim());
  return exp.every((v,i)=> head[i]===v);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotency and retry behavior?
- How would you monitor failures and implement dead-letter handling?
